WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.096
A monotonic stack maintains elements in strictly increasing or decreasing order as you scan an array.

00:00:08.096 --> 00:00:14.188
Before pushing a new element, pop every top element that is worse and older.

00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:20.440
Each element is pushed once and popped at most once, guaranteeing linear time.

00:00:20.708 --> 00:00:27.205
When searching for the next greater element to the right, keep a decreasing stack of unresolved indices.

00:00:27.205 --> 00:00:33.326
As soon as a larger value arrives, it resolves and pops all smaller items at the top of the stack.

00:00:33.326 --> 00:00:37.948
The item remaining below becomes the nearest greater neighbor on the left.

00:00:38.208 --> 00:00:45.613
A monotonic deque extends the stack by also evicting expired indices from the front as a window slides.

00:00:45.613 --> 00:00:52.729
Maintain indices in decreasing order of value so the front always holds the current window maximum.

00:00:52.729 --> 00:00:58.408
Sliding window minimum and maximum queries then run in amortized O(1) per step.

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:07.308
In histogram and grid problems, each bar wants to know how far left and right it can extend without meeting a shorter bar.

00:01:07.308 --> 00:01:12.338
A monotonic stack finds both boundaries for every bar in a single pass.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:17.438
Multiply each bar's height by its maximal span to find the largest area.
